## Create a builder

A project builder defines the source capabilities available to a Moth project
and turns validated compiled assemblies into application artefacts. The
high-level SDK is the `moth-builder` crate. It uses the canonical `moth`
compiler and build services internally.

The four values used by most builders are:

- `BuilderDefinition`, the immutable source-visible capability definition
- `CompiledProject`, the successful compiler/build result exposed through safe
  owner-bound views
- `BuildContext`, the validated lowering, resource and output services for one
  build
- `BuildArtifacts`, the output records returned to Moth's central output owner

`ProjectBuilder` connects them through `definition` and `build`.

### A minimal package builder

This builder supplies one Moth source package and one stateless Rust host
package, then compiles the authored project package facade to Wasm.

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    use std::path::PathBuf;

    use moth_builder::{
        BuildArtifacts, BuildContext, BuilderApiVersion, BuilderDefinition,
        CompiledProject, Error, ProjectBuilder, SourcePackage,
    };
    use moth_host::{HostPackage, host_binding};

    #[moth_host::host_function]
    fn has_prefix(text: &str, prefix: &str) -> bool {
        text.starts_with(prefix)
    }

    fn platform_package() -> HostPackage<()> {
        HostPackage::stateless("@platform")
            .function(host_binding!(has_prefix))
    }

    struct GameBuilder {
        package_root: PathBuf,
    }

    impl ProjectBuilder for GameBuilder {
        fn definition(&self) -> Result<BuilderDefinition, Error> {
            BuilderDefinition::new(
                "com.example.game",
                BuilderApiVersion::V1,
            )
            .source_package(
                SourcePackage::new("@game")
                    .from_dir(&self.package_root),
            )
            .host_package(platform_package())
            .moth_templates()
            .markdown()
            .finish()
        }

        fn build(
            &self,
            project: &CompiledProject,
            context: &mut BuildContext,
        ) -> Result<BuildArtifacts, Error> {
            let package = project.require_package()?;
            let wasm = context.compile_wasm(package)?;

            let mut artifacts = BuildArtifacts::new();
            artifacts.add("game.wasm", wasm)?;
            Ok(artifacts)
        }
    }
]

`require_package` returns the owner-bound assembly for the authored project
facade. The facade selects its public surface through ordinary Moth visibility
rules and does not activate normal-root `start` work.

`compile_wasm` requests the canonical compiler/backend handoff for that
assembly. The builder does not pass HIR, borrow facts, numeric proofs or type
stores itself.

`BuildArtifacts::add` reserves a relative output. It records the artefact but
does not write it. The build system validates conflicts, writes files, owns the
manifest and removes stale output only after a successful replacement build.

### Use the canonical project-tool driver

A normal standalone builder executable delegates its command shell to
`moth-builder`:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    fn main() -> moth_builder::Result<()> {
        moth_builder::run(GameBuilder::new())
    }
]

The driver keeps Moth project tools consistent. Core commands such as `build`,
`check`, `dev` and `new` retain their Moth meanings and argument rules.
Builder-specific commands live under the reserved `builder` command scope.
A builder cannot replace a core command with different semantics.

A builder may leave an optional command capability unsupported. For example, a
builder with no development runtime reports that `dev` is unavailable rather
than redefining it. Supported `dev` implementations plug into the canonical
build/watch lifecycle instead of owning another source compiler or config path.

### Embed the lower-level API

Applications that already own their process, such as an engine or editor, can
use the request API directly instead of the CLI driver:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    use moth_builder::{BuildRequest, build_project};

    let request = BuildRequest::new("game-project");
    let result = build_project(&builder, request)?;

    inspect(&result);
    result.write_outputs()?;
]

Build and write remain separate operations. A failed semantic compilation never
supplies a partial `CompiledProject` to `ProjectBuilder::build`. A failed build
never authorises stale-output cleanup as though a replacement succeeded.

### Builder identity and compatibility

Every builder declares a stable `BuilderId` through `BuilderDefinition::new`
and one `BuilderApiVersion`. The ID is semantic ownership, not a Rust type name,
filesystem path or closed compiler enum. It participates in manifest ownership,
cache compatibility and diagnostics.

The builder's own release version is separate. It may be recorded for
diagnostics and tooling, but changing it alone does not invalidate artefacts
whose actual capability contracts are unchanged.

The active driver chooses the builder before `config.moth` is compiled.
Project source cannot dynamically load or switch builders. Builder
configuration describes the already selected builder.

### Define once, validate once

The fluent definition API is the supported way to construct the compiler
capability surface. `finish` validates the complete registration and freezes
the result.

It rejects duplicate or conflicting package roots, directives, namespaces,
config fields, root purposes and compiler-owned names. It also validates the
builder ID, API version and target implementation contracts.

Definition validation uses structured builder diagnostics. Rust-item problems
that can be proven by `#[moth_host::host_function]` or
`#[moth_host::host_type]` fail earlier as Rust compile-time diagnostics.

The definition is static policy. Mutable provider caches, resolution tables,
string/path identity construction and per-build source state remain owned by
the Moth compiler/build session.

### Build-time and runtime dependencies

A builder executable normally depends on `moth-builder` and `moth-host`.
`moth-builder` depends on the canonical `moth` compiler.

A deployed application that only runs already-built Wasm can depend on
`moth-host` without pulling in `moth-builder` or the compiler. It uses the same
host-package declaration functions as the build tool, so semantic descriptors
and runtime implementations do not drift into separate copies.
